$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 03/Practice
::HC-W3-P01::A cervical-screening campaign targets all women aged 25 to 49 with the same message despite major differences in prior screening, urban access and fear of the procedure. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=actionable audience segmentation#Correct. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~meaning failure in translation#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~excessive health-literacy demand#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~digital access and privacy inequity#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
}

::HC-W3-P02::A cervical-screening campaign targets all women aged 25 to 49 with the same message despite major differences in prior screening, urban access and fear of the procedure. What is the strongest next response? {
=Segment by decision-relevant differences such as prior screening, access and concerns, then tailor strategy accordingly.#Correct. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~Conduct audience testing of the adapted language and revise for locally understood meaning rather than literal equivalence.#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~Use plain language, prioritise essential actions and explain unavoidable technical terms.#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
~Provide safe alternative channels and assess whether phone sharing could expose sensitive information.#Not the best answer. Demography alone does not identify the communication barrier.
}

::HC-W3-P03::A district translates an English leaflet word-for-word into Luganda, but users interpret a key phrase as meaning the vaccine causes the disease. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=meaning failure in translation#Correct. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~excessive health-literacy demand#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~digital access and privacy inequity#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~unrepresentative formative research#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
}

::HC-W3-P04::A district translates an English leaflet word-for-word into Luganda, but users interpret a key phrase as meaning the vaccine causes the disease. What is the strongest next response? {
=Conduct audience testing of the adapted language and revise for locally understood meaning rather than literal equivalence.#Correct. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~Use plain language, prioritise essential actions and explain unavoidable technical terms.#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~Provide safe alternative channels and assess whether phone sharing could expose sensitive information.#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
~Recruit adolescents and other affected subgroups through safe methods before generalising the finding.#Not the best answer. Semantic accuracy is not guaranteed by literal translation.
}

::HC-W3-P05::A diabetes handout uses terms such as glycaemic control and microvascular complications for newly diagnosed patients. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=excessive health-literacy demand#Correct. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~digital access and privacy inequity#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~unrepresentative formative research#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~stereotyping disguised as persona design#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
}

::HC-W3-P06::A diabetes handout uses terms such as glycaemic control and microvascular complications for newly diagnosed patients. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use plain language, prioritise essential actions and explain unavoidable technical terms.#Correct. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~Provide safe alternative channels and assess whether phone sharing could expose sensitive information.#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~Recruit adolescents and other affected subgroups through safe methods before generalising the finding.#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
~Ground personas in formative evidence and describe specific decisions and constraints rather than fixed character traits.#Not the best answer. The material demands specialised vocabulary that many users may not understand.
}

::HC-W3-P07::A maternal-health app assumes every woman has private access to a smartphone, but many share phones with partners. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=digital access and privacy inequity#Correct. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~unrepresentative formative research#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~stereotyping disguised as persona design#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~accessibility failure#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
}

::HC-W3-P08::A maternal-health app assumes every woman has private access to a smartphone, but many share phones with partners. What is the strongest next response? {
=Provide safe alternative channels and assess whether phone sharing could expose sensitive information.#Correct. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~Recruit adolescents and other affected subgroups through safe methods before generalising the finding.#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~Ground personas in formative evidence and describe specific decisions and constraints rather than fixed character traits.#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
~Redesign visual hierarchy, contrast, type size and alternative formats, then test usability with affected users.#Not the best answer. Access is shaped by ownership, privacy and power, not only network coverage.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 03/Mastery
::HC-W3-M01::Focus groups include only local leaders, and the team concludes that adolescents have no concerns about contraception services. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unrepresentative formative research#Correct. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~stereotyping disguised as persona design#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~accessibility failure#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~inadequate comprehension check#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
}

::HC-W3-M02::Focus groups include only local leaders, and the team concludes that adolescents have no concerns about contraception services. What is the strongest next response? {
=Recruit adolescents and other affected subgroups through safe methods before generalising the finding.#Correct. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~Ground personas in formative evidence and describe specific decisions and constraints rather than fixed character traits.#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~Redesign visual hierarchy, contrast, type size and alternative formats, then test usability with affected users.#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
~Use teach-back or demonstration to test how people interpret and use the instructions.#Not the best answer. Leaders cannot substitute for every audience perspective.
}

::HC-W3-M03::A team creates a persona called the resistant rural mother based only on staff assumptions. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=stereotyping disguised as persona design#Correct. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~accessibility failure#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~inadequate comprehension check#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~culture used as a residual explanation#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
}

::HC-W3-M04::A team creates a persona called the resistant rural mother based only on staff assumptions. What is the strongest next response? {
=Ground personas in formative evidence and describe specific decisions and constraints rather than fixed character traits.#Correct. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~Redesign visual hierarchy, contrast, type size and alternative formats, then test usability with affected users.#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~Use teach-back or demonstration to test how people interpret and use the instructions.#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
~Investigate the concrete social expectations, histories and decision roles that influence contraceptive choices.#Not the best answer. Personas should synthesise evidence, not formalise stereotypes.
}

::HC-W3-M05::A poster is technically accurate but uses very small text and colour contrast that people with low vision struggle to read. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=accessibility failure#Correct. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~inadequate comprehension check#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~culture used as a residual explanation#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~coverage bias in digital listening#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
}

::HC-W3-M06::A poster is technically accurate but uses very small text and colour contrast that people with low vision struggle to read. What is the strongest next response? {
=Redesign visual hierarchy, contrast, type size and alternative formats, then test usability with affected users.#Correct. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~Use teach-back or demonstration to test how people interpret and use the instructions.#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~Investigate the concrete social expectations, histories and decision roles that influence contraceptive choices.#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
~Treat online data as a signal and triangulate with offline community listening and representative data.#Not the best answer. Accurate content can still be inaccessible.
}

::HC-W3-M07::A health worker asks patients whether they understand a medicine label and nearly everyone says yes, but dosing errors remain common. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=inadequate comprehension check#Correct. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~culture used as a residual explanation#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~coverage bias in digital listening#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~programme-centred definition of reach#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
}

::HC-W3-M08::A health worker asks patients whether they understand a medicine label and nearly everyone says yes, but dosing errors remain common. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use teach-back or demonstration to test how people interpret and use the instructions.#Correct. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~Investigate the concrete social expectations, histories and decision roles that influence contraceptive choices.#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~Treat online data as a signal and triangulate with offline community listening and representative data.#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
~Study work patterns and use channels and times that fit the community rather than blaming the audience.#Not the best answer. Yes-no questions are weak evidence of understanding.
}

::HC-W3-M09::A campaign explains non-use of family planning as culture without identifying any specific norm, role or belief. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=culture used as a residual explanation#Correct. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~coverage bias in digital listening#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~programme-centred definition of reach#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~language accessibility gap#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
}

::HC-W3-M10::A campaign explains non-use of family planning as culture without identifying any specific norm, role or belief. What is the strongest next response? {
=Investigate the concrete social expectations, histories and decision roles that influence contraceptive choices.#Correct. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~Treat online data as a signal and triangulate with offline community listening and representative data.#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~Study work patterns and use channels and times that fit the community rather than blaming the audience.#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
~Add language options or trusted interpreters and test whether users can safely access them.#Not the best answer. Culture is not an explanatory variable until the relevant mechanism is specified.
}

::HC-W3-M11::Online comments suggest strong vaccine confidence, but the district has many households with no reliable internet access. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=coverage bias in digital listening#Correct. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~programme-centred definition of reach#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~language accessibility gap#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~confounding and contextual explanation#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
}

::HC-W3-M12::Online comments suggest strong vaccine confidence, but the district has many households with no reliable internet access. What is the strongest next response? {
=Treat online data as a signal and triangulate with offline community listening and representative data.#Correct. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~Study work patterns and use channels and times that fit the community rather than blaming the audience.#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~Add language options or trusted interpreters and test whether users can safely access them.#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
~Examine phone ownership, language, data cost, disability and service relevance before attributing the difference to schooling.#Not the best answer. Platform users are not necessarily representative of the target population.
}

::HC-W3-M13::A programme labels a fishing community hard to reach because members rarely attend daytime meetings. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=programme-centred definition of reach#Correct. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~language accessibility gap#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~confounding and contextual explanation#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~expert validation substituted for audience testing#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
}

::HC-W3-M14::A programme labels a fishing community hard to reach because members rarely attend daytime meetings. What is the strongest next response? {
=Study work patterns and use channels and times that fit the community rather than blaming the audience.#Correct. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~Add language options or trusted interpreters and test whether users can safely access them.#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~Examine phone ownership, language, data cost, disability and service relevance before attributing the difference to schooling.#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
~Retain technical review but also test comprehension, relevance and usability with intended users.#Not the best answer. Reach is partly a design property of the programme.
}

::HC-W3-M15::A pregnancy hotline is offered only in the national language in a multilingual refugee settlement. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=language accessibility gap#Correct. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~confounding and contextual explanation#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~expert validation substituted for audience testing#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~selection bias in audience preference data#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
}

::HC-W3-M16::A pregnancy hotline is offered only in the national language in a multilingual refugee settlement. What is the strongest next response? {
=Add language options or trusted interpreters and test whether users can safely access them.#Correct. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~Examine phone ownership, language, data cost, disability and service relevance before attributing the difference to schooling.#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~Retain technical review but also test comprehension, relevance and usability with intended users.#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
~Do not generalise the preference estimate; recruit through multiple pathways or use a representative sampling frame.#Not the best answer. Availability of a hotline does not equal accessibility.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 03/Institutional
::HC-W3-I01::A team finds that women with less schooling use antenatal SMS less often and concludes education causes low engagement. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=confounding and contextual explanation#Correct. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~expert validation substituted for audience testing#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~selection bias in audience preference data#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~actionable audience segmentation#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
}

::HC-W3-I02::A team finds that women with less schooling use antenatal SMS less often and concludes education causes low engagement. What is the strongest next response? {
=Examine phone ownership, language, data cost, disability and service relevance before attributing the difference to schooling.#Correct. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~Retain technical review but also test comprehension, relevance and usability with intended users.#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~Do not generalise the preference estimate; recruit through multiple pathways or use a representative sampling frame.#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
~Segment by decision-relevant differences such as prior screening, access and concerns, then tailor strategy accordingly.#Not the best answer. Education may proxy multiple structural differences.
}

::HC-W3-I03::A pre-test is conducted with health professionals instead of the intended low-literacy audience. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=expert validation substituted for audience testing#Correct. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~selection bias in audience preference data#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~actionable audience segmentation#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~meaning failure in translation#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
}

::HC-W3-I04::A pre-test is conducted with health professionals instead of the intended low-literacy audience. What is the strongest next response? {
=Retain technical review but also test comprehension, relevance and usability with intended users.#Correct. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~Do not generalise the preference estimate; recruit through multiple pathways or use a representative sampling frame.#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~Segment by decision-relevant differences such as prior screening, access and concerns, then tailor strategy accordingly.#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
~Conduct audience testing of the adapted language and revise for locally understood meaning rather than literal equivalence.#Not the best answer. Experts can assess accuracy but not how the audience will interpret the product.
}

::HC-W3-I05::A survey reports that 80 percent of respondents prefer WhatsApp, but interviews were conducted through a WhatsApp recruitment link. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=selection bias in audience preference data#Correct. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~actionable audience segmentation#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~meaning failure in translation#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~excessive health-literacy demand#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
}

::HC-W3-I06::A survey reports that 80 percent of respondents prefer WhatsApp, but interviews were conducted through a WhatsApp recruitment link. What is the strongest next response? {
=Do not generalise the preference estimate; recruit through multiple pathways or use a representative sampling frame.#Correct. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~Segment by decision-relevant differences such as prior screening, access and concerns, then tailor strategy accordingly.#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~Conduct audience testing of the adapted language and revise for locally understood meaning rather than literal equivalence.#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
~Use plain language, prioritise essential actions and explain unavoidable technical terms.#Not the best answer. The recruitment method preferentially selected WhatsApp users.
}

